        "title": "What is Income Tax — Why Do You Have to Pay It",
        "content": "Income tax is the money the government deducts from your income. It's the contribution for running the country — roads, hospitals, schools, defense, everything runs on this.\r\nIn India, income tax is a direct tax — meaning it's deducted directly from your income. Every financial year (April 1 to March 31), your total income is calculated, and tax is applied based on \"slabs\" — more income = more tax.\r\nImportant question for students: \"Will I have to pay tax?\"\r\nAnswer: If your annual income is less than ₹2.5 lakh (old regime) or less than ₹4 lakh (new regime), you pay ZERO tax. And in the new regime, with the Section 87A rebate, even taxable income up to ₹12 lakh is zero tax — salary up to ₹12.75 lakh (after ₹75,000 standard deduction, taxable ₹12 lakh) = nil tax.\r\nMost students earn less than this, so they don't formally have to pay tax. But filing ITR is still beneficial — for refunds, for loans, for visas.\r\nIncome Tax Slabs FY 2025-26:\r\nIncome Range (Annual) Old Regime Tax Rate New Regime Tax Rate\r\n0-₹2,50,000 0% 0%\r\n₹2,50,001-₹4,00,000 5% 0%\r\n₹4,00,001-₹5,00,000 5% 5%\r\n₹5,00,001-₹8,00,000 20% 5%\r\n₹8,00,001-₹10,00,000 20% 10%\r\n₹10,00,001-₹12,00,000 30% 10%\r\n₹12,00,001-₹16,00,000 30% 15%\r\n₹16,00,001-₹20,00,000 30% 20%\r\n₹20,00,001-₹24,00,000 30% 25%\r\nAbove ₹24,00,000 30% 30%\r\nNote: Salary of ₹12.75 lakh (₹75,000 standard deduction minus) = taxable ₹12 lakh = nil tax in new regime after 87A rebate.\r\nImportant: Income up to ₹5 lakh is zero tax with Section 87A rebate (old regime). In the new regime, with 87A rebate, taxable income up to ₹12 lakh is ZERO tax (from FY 2025-26). A student's salary of ₹25,000/month = ₹3 lakh/year = effectively zero tax. But do file ITR!\r\nMarginal Relief Note: If taxable income is slightly above ₹12 lakh (like ₹12.1L-₹12.3L zone), don't worry. Marginal relief ensures you don't pay more tax than someone earning ₹12 lakh. The excess tax over the ₹12 lakh taxpayer's tax is waived.\r\n\r\nNew Regime is Default: From FY 2023-24, new regime is default. You have to actively choose old regime if you want deductions. Most students with no investments → new regime is better.\r\n\r\nKey Insight for Students:\r\n- Salary ₹25,000/month = ₹3 lakh/year = ZERO tax\r\n- Salary ₹50,000/month = ₹6 lakh/year = ZERO tax (new regime with 87A)\r\n- Salary ₹1,00,000/month = ₹12 lakh/year = ZERO tax (new regime with 87A)\r\n- But FILE ITR anyway — it's proof of income for loans, visas, future reference",
