        "content": "TDS (Tax Deducted at Source) means — tax is deducted before you even receive the money. For example, if FD interest is ₹50,000, the bank deducts ₹5,000 TDS, and you get ₹45,000. This is advance tax — the government wants the money now, not later.\r\nHow to Check TDS:\r\nForm 26AS: On incometax.gov.in — shows all TDS details. Updated every quarter.\r\nAIS (Annual Information Statement): New system — detailed income breakdown. Banks, employers, mutual funds all report.\r\nIf excess TDS has been deducted, file ITR and claim a refund! Refund comes to your bank account in 3-6 months.\r\nCommon TDS Scenarios:\r\nSalary: Employer deducts TDS (as per Form 16).\r\nFD interest: 10% TDS above ₹40,000.\r\nFreelance: Client deducts 10% TDS above ₹30,000/transaction.\r\nRent: Tenant deducts 5% TDS above ₹50,000/month.",
