        "content": "Which is Better?\r\nComparison:\r\nFactor Old Regime New Regime\r\nBasic exemption ₹2.5L ₹4L\r\n87A rebate (zero tax) Up to ₹5L income Up to ₹12L income\r\nDeductions (80C etc.) Allowed Most NOT allowed\r\nStandard deduction ₹50,000 ₹75,000\r\nTax rates Higher slabs Lower slabs\r\nRule of Thumb for Students:\r\nIf you're investing in 80C (SIP, PPF, FD) → Old Regime is better because deductions reduce taxable income.\r\nIf you're not doing any investment → New Regime is better because of lower tax rates + higher exemption.\r\nCalculator Example with ₹6 lakh salary:\r\nOld Regime: ₹6,00,000 - ₹50,000 (standard) - ₹1,50,000 (80C ELSS) = ₹4,00,000 taxable. Tax = ₹7,500. 87A rebate = ₹7,500. Tax = ZERO!\r\nNew Regime: ₹6,00,000 - ₹75,000 = ₹5,25,000 taxable. Tax = ₹6,250 (5% on amount above ₹4L). 87A rebate (up to ₹12L) = ₹6,250. Tax = ZERO!\r\nBoth give zero tax. But if you invest ₹1.5 lakh in 80C → old regime builds a habit + creates wealth. New regime with no investment = no wealth.\r\nDecision Flowchart:\r\nAre you investing in 80C?\r\n    ↓ YES → Old Regime (deductions + lower taxable income)\r\n    ↓ NO → Is your income below ₹12L?\r\n        ↓ YES → New Regime (lower rates, no deductions needed)\r\n        ↓ NO → Calculate both, choose whichever gives lower tax\r\nCalculator: Free calculator available on incometax.gov.in. Compare both.",
